         <item>
          <widget class="QLabel" name="title">
           <property name="font">
            <font>
             <family>Arial</family>
             <pointsize>9</pointsize>
             <italic>false</italic>
             <bold>false</bold>
            </font>
           </property>
           <property name="whatsThis">
            <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p align=&quot;justify&quot;&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
           </property>
           <property name="styleSheet">
            <string notr="true">QFrame[objectName= &quot;frame&quot;]{background-color: rgb(220,220,220);}</string>
           </property>
           <property name="text">
            <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The software Step (1) processes polarised optical microscopy scans and Step (2) stacks multi-modal images as pyramidal OME-TIFF format that can be opened in OMERO web-server. Step (1) pyramid level is specified in '../*_pyramid_sizes.csv' containing the pixel calibration. All inputs (in proprietary format) and outputs (in open-source) can be opened in QuPath software and Evident VS200 software suite. Step (2) pre-requisite is doing image alignment (or registration) in ImageJ-Fiji BigWarp plugin or QuPath Warpy extension that supports OME-TIFF. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Step (1) inputs need to follow the acquisition sequences of QUT VS200 petrographic slide scanner by Acevedo and Kamber (2023). The current source code repository (https://github.com/marcoaaz/cube_converter) updates the original scripts (https://github.com/marcoaaz/Acevedo-Kamber) as a pyvips (https://github.com/libvips/pyvips) implementation. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
           </property>
           <property name="alignment">
            <set>Qt::AlignCenter</set>
           </property>
           <property name="wordWrap">
            <bool>true</bool>
           </property>
          </widget>
